# chora's manifest (#963).
#
# chora drives whatever device it is started on, so it asks for any PCI
# class, with the three operations a driver needs: DMA, its registers and its
# interrupt.
#
# 🔑 And it is given only what its parent may hand on.  exec_load() cuts what
# this file asks for to the parent's `delegatable' lines (#743), so a chora
# holds the one class its parent hands on and nothing else: chora_test hands
# on the network class.  Two choras started by two parents hold what each was
# given, and this file grants nothing by itself.

name chora
required 5 any 0x7

# No `delegatable' lines: a chora starts nothing.
